CellScript v0.13.2: closing the 0.13 stable line with strict CKB acceptance evidence
CellScript v0.13.2 is now ready as the stable closing release for the 0.13 line.
This release is not only a compiler update. The main focus of 0.13.2 is release hardening: making the syntax surface more stable, keeping Cell effects audit-visible, and strengthening the CKB acceptance evidence behind the bundled production examples.
What changed in 0.13?
The 0.13 line introduced and stabilised several important areas:
bounded stack-backed Vec<T: FixedWidth> helper support;
canonical action/update syntax with named outputs, where proof blocks, and explicit move state edges;
visible lock-boundary data sources through protected, witness, and fixed-width lock_args;
stdlib lifecycle and Cell metadata helpers that lower to explicit verifier obligations;
syntax-combination audit gates across parser, formatter, type checking, lowering, metadata, and codegen;
stronger local CKB production acceptance for the bundled examples.
A core design rule in this release is:
Sugar may shorten source code, but it must not hide Cell movement, ownership, or authorisation.
For example, helpers such as lifecycle transfer/claim/settle patterns and Cell metadata helpers are intended to be audit-visible shorthand. They lower to explicit verifier obligations rather than silently inventing protocol policy.
Stateful CKB acceptance
The biggest release-evidence improvement in v0.13.2 is the new strict stateful acceptance layer.
Previously, the acceptance suite already covered individual production actions and builder-backed local CKB transaction checks. In 0.13.2, the stateful runner now exercises committed multi-transaction flows where live outputs from earlier actions become real inputs to later actions.
The current release evidence includes:
7 production bundled examples
43/43 production acceptance actions covered
16 builder-backed lock valid/invalid spend cases
27 stateful local CKB scenarios
46 committed stateful steps
7 end-to-end business-flow scenarios
20 stateful action-branch scenarios
The stateful scenarios cover the main bundled example families:
token.mint-transfer-mint-merge-burn
nft.mint-list-transfer-by-listing
timelock.create-lock-lock-asset-request-release-execute
launch.launch-token-then-mint
amm.seed-add-swap-remove
vesting.create-config-grant-revoke
multisig.create-propose-sign-sign-execute
Additional stateful action-branch scenarios cover the remaining production acceptance actions not already exercised by those end-to-end flows.
This means the release evidence is no longer just “the examples compile” or “a few happy paths run”. The gate now checks that every production acceptance action appears in the stateful report, with no missing action IDs or missing action artifacts.
What each committed step records
Each stateful step records concrete execution evidence, including:
dry-run evidence;
committed transaction evidence;
measured cycles;
consensus-serialized transaction size;
occupied capacity;
capacity checks;
consumed-input liveness checks;
output liveness checks;
malformed transaction rejection where applicable.
Under-capacity outputs are rejected by the release gate.
The release-facing command is:
./scripts/cellscript_ckb_release_gate.sh full
This full gate includes compiler/tooling checks, syntax-combination CI audits, VS Code validation, documentation-boundary checks, builder-backed local CKB acceptance, and stateful scenario/action coverage.
What this proves
The current evidence proves that the bundled production CellScript examples compile under the CKB target profile and that the generated artifacts can participate in concrete local CKB devnet transactions.
More importantly, it proves that the acceptance gate observes actual Cell lifecycle behaviour:
valid transactions dry-run and commit;
live outputs can be handed into later transactions;
consumed inputs are no longer live;
expected outputs remain live;
malformed variants are rejected;
cycles, size, and occupied capacity are measured.
In other words, the bundled examples are no longer merely compile-time demonstrations. They now have committed stateful CKB protocol-flow evidence behind them.
What this does not claim though
CellScript v0.13.2 is not claiming:
external security audit closure;
mainnet-value certification;
exhaustive state-space verification;
formal verification;
hidden signer authority;
hidden sighash defaults;
full generic maps;
Cell-backed generic collection ownership;
declarative capacity or since/header policy.
Those remain future work or intentionally out of scope.
In particular, witness Address is still just decoded witness data. It is not a cryptographic authorisation proof by itself. First-class signer values and explicit sighash verification need stronger CKB binding semantics and separate release evidence before they should become stable source syntax.
Next
The 0.13 implementation scope is now closed. The remaining CKB semantic work is being kept explicit for the next line, including areas such as structured WitnessArgs, ScriptGroup conformance fixtures, broader Source-view semantics, and explicit signature-verification primitives.
As always, comments, criticism, and review are very welcome.
Thank you to everyone who has been following the project and giving feedback.
